1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,459
The inverter of Lesson 17 has eight
switching states:

2
00:00:04,499 --> 00:00:08,685
six fixed voltage vectors, 60° apart, and
two zeros.

3
00:00:08,725 --> 00:00:11,591
Yet the motor needs a voltage vector of
any length,

4
00:00:11,631 --> 00:00:13,758
at any angle, turning smoothly.

5
00:00:13,798 --> 00:00:18,525
The trick: in each 50 µs period, split the
time between the two nearest

6
00:00:18,565 --> 00:00:23,624
states and zero. On average, you get
exactly the vector you asked for.

7
00:00:23,664 --> 00:00:25,279
Today: space-vector PWM,

8
00:00:25,319 --> 00:00:30,165
and how the same timer tells the ADC when
to read the currents.

9
00:00:31,265 --> 00:00:33,921
First, the answers from Lesson 17.

10
00:00:33,961 --> 00:00:37,254
One: 24 V × 500 ns × 20 kHz.

11
00:00:37,294 --> 00:00:44,083
Each leg loses 0.24 V on average, against
its current.

12
00:00:44,123 --> 00:00:48,596
Two: only the two zero vectors, 000 and
111, with every terminal on the same rail,

13
00:00:48,636 --> 00:00:51,583
put zero volts between every pair of
terminals.

14
00:00:51,623 --> 00:00:57,064
Three: a sixth of third harmonic flattens
the peaks just enough.

15
00:00:57,104 --> 00:01:01,543
2/√3, 15.5 % more, exactly what min–max
gives.

16
00:01:02,643 --> 00:01:05,375
Here are the eight states as space
vectors:

17
00:01:05,415 --> 00:01:09,128
each active one is two-thirds of the bus,
16 V long.

18
00:01:09,168 --> 00:01:14,032
Project a vector onto a phase axis to get
that phase's voltage: with only

19
00:01:14,072 --> 00:01:19,537
the upper switch of phase a on (100),
that's 16 V on a, and −8 V on b and c.

20
00:01:19,577 --> 00:01:22,978
Six active vectors make six sectors of
60°.

21
00:01:23,018 --> 00:01:27,780
Divide the reference's angle by 60° to
find its sector.

22
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:32,223
This one sits in sector 1, between the
first two active vectors, 100 and 110.

23
00:01:33,323 --> 00:01:37,132
The winding's inductance filters anything
faster than a period,

24
00:01:37,172 --> 00:01:41,137
so what moves the current is the average:
the volt-seconds.

25
00:01:41,177 --> 00:01:46,221
So ask for the same volt-seconds: time on
the first neighbour times its vector, plus

26
00:01:46,261 --> 00:01:51,583
time on the second times its vector. The
zero vectors fill the rest of the period.

27
00:01:51,623 --> 00:01:56,731
Split the reference along the two sector
edges. The triangle gives

28
00:01:56,771 --> 00:02:01,878
the first dwell time: √3 times the
reference over the bus voltage,

29
00:02:01,918 --> 00:02:07,582
times the period, times sin(60° − θ). The
second uses sin θ instead.

30
00:02:07,622 --> 00:02:15,127
Take Lesson 17's operating point, 8.59 V,
caught at 20°: the first neighbour

31
00:02:15,167 --> 00:02:22,716
gets 19.9 µs, the second 10.6, and the
zero vectors the remaining 19.5.

32
00:02:23,816 --> 00:02:29,592
Now order them in time. Start with all
lower switches on (000), switch leg a up,

33
00:02:29,632 --> 00:02:35,736
then leg b, then leg c, and come back the
same way: states 0, 1, 2, 7, 2, 1, 0.

34
00:02:35,776 --> 00:02:39,498
Each step changes just one leg: six
transitions per period,

35
00:02:39,538 --> 00:02:43,978
one on and one off per leg, the minimum
when every leg switches.

36
00:02:44,018 --> 00:02:46,642
The zero time is split equally between
all-low

37
00:02:46,682 --> 00:02:49,653
(000) at the edges and all-high (111) in
the middle,

38
00:02:49,693 --> 00:02:53,644
so the pulses are centred, just like the
carrier in Lesson 17.

39
00:02:53,684 --> 00:02:57,965
Add up the volt-seconds as the period
runs: along the first vector,

40
00:02:58,005 --> 00:03:02,738
then the second, a pause at all-high, then
the second and the first again.

41
00:03:02,778 --> 00:03:06,374
Divided by T, they end exactly on the
reference.

42
00:03:07,474 --> 00:03:12,310
How long can the reference get? The two
dwell times must fit inside the period.

43
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:19,097
That holds on and inside the circle
inscribed in the hexagon. Its radius

44
00:03:19,137 --> 00:03:26,096
is 16 V × cos 30°, the bus voltage over
√3: 13.9 V of phase amplitude.

45
00:03:26,136 --> 00:03:31,555
On that circle, in the middle of each
sector, the zero time drops to zero.

46
00:03:31,595 --> 00:03:37,635
That's 15.5 % more than sine-triangle's
12 V, and the full 24 V line-to-line.

47
00:03:37,675 --> 00:03:42,708
Beyond it is overmodulation: the vector
gets cut back to the hexagon,

48
00:03:42,748 --> 00:03:48,321
adding low-order harmonics, up to six-step
with a 15.3 V fundamental.

49
00:03:49,421 --> 00:03:52,508
Plot one leg's duty from the seven-step
pattern over

50
00:03:52,548 --> 00:03:55,974
an electrical cycle, and a familiar saddle
appears.

51
00:03:56,014 --> 00:04:00,186
Lay Lesson 17's carrier with min–max
injection on top:

52
00:04:00,226 --> 00:04:04,642
identical, for all 4000 references our
code checked.

53
00:04:04,682 --> 00:04:09,279
Why? With the zeros split equally, the
largest duty gets half the zero

54
00:04:09,319 --> 00:04:13,783
time plus both active times; the smallest,
half the zero time alone.

55
00:04:13,823 --> 00:04:17,893
Together: exactly one period, so the
pulses are centred.

56
00:04:17,933 --> 00:04:22,314
In carrier terms, the largest and smallest
references add to zero:

57
00:04:22,354 --> 00:04:26,070
the min–max rule. One modulator, built two
ways.

58
00:04:27,170 --> 00:04:30,264
Now the currents. Low-cost drives put a
small shunt

59
00:04:30,304 --> 00:04:34,043
resistor under each lower switch, to the
negative rail.

60
00:04:34,083 --> 00:04:36,738
Each shunt carries its phase current only
while

61
00:04:36,778 --> 00:04:39,891
that leg's lower switch, or its diode,
conducts.

62
00:04:39,931 --> 00:04:43,380
In 000, all three lower switches conduct,

63
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:49,396
so all three shunts show their currents at
once, as −R_sh times i.

64
00:04:49,436 --> 00:04:51,987
Its middle is farthest from the switching
edges,

65
00:04:52,027 --> 00:04:54,654
with their ringing and amplifier settling.

66
00:04:54,694 --> 00:04:57,049
The timer marks that instant for free:

67
00:04:57,089 --> 00:05:02,127
its counter turns around at zero there,
and that event triggers the ADC.

68
00:05:03,227 --> 00:05:07,666
There's a bonus. Here's phase a's current
at the operating point,

69
00:05:07,706 --> 00:05:11,167
simulated edge by edge, with 2.8 A of
ripple.

70
00:05:11,207 --> 00:05:14,473
The pulses are symmetric about the middle
of each period,

71
00:05:14,513 --> 00:05:18,874
so the ripple crosses its average in the
middle of both zero vectors.

72
00:05:18,914 --> 00:05:24,555
So the samples sit on the average, off by
at most 0.08 A over a cycle.

73
00:05:24,595 --> 00:05:29,477
At an arbitrary instant, the error could
reach 1.25 A.

74
00:05:30,577 --> 00:05:34,650
At high modulation, though, the zero
vectors shrink.

75
00:05:34,690 --> 00:05:41,457
At 95 % of the linear limit, the all-low
vector (000) lasts as little as 1.25 µs.

76
00:05:41,497 --> 00:05:46,136
But a shunt reading needs more: dead time,
about a microsecond of

77
00:05:46,176 --> 00:05:51,215
ringing and settling each side, and the
ADC's sampling. Say 3 µs.

78
00:05:51,255 --> 00:05:55,166
The way out: the currents add to zero, so
two are enough.

79
00:05:55,206 --> 00:06:00,488
Skip the leg with the largest duty; the
other two keep at least 4.4 µs.

80
00:06:00,528 --> 00:06:05,063
Two-shunt boards can't choose, so they cap
the modulation or estimate the

81
00:06:05,103 --> 00:06:10,013
missing sample. A single DC-link shunt
reads during the active vectors instead,

82
00:06:10,053 --> 00:06:13,060
shifting edges when those are too short.

83
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:18,361
Your turn. The exercise script is the
whole modulator in ten lines:

84
00:06:18,401 --> 00:06:23,445
it finds the sector, computes the two
dwell times, and returns the duties.

85
00:06:23,485 --> 00:06:25,831
Run it and check this lesson's numbers.

86
00:06:25,871 --> 00:06:31,082
Then push the reference past the circle,
and watch the zero time go negative.

87
00:06:32,182 --> 00:06:37,742
On a microcontroller, the modulator is a
timer in centre-aligned mode.

88
00:06:37,782 --> 00:06:43,582
It counts up and down: at 100 MHz, 2500
counts each way make 20 kHz.

89
00:06:43,622 --> 00:06:48,628
Each leg's compare register gets 2500 × (1
− d); the upper switch is

90
00:06:48,668 --> 00:06:53,925
on while the count is above it, with dead
time added by the timer.

91
00:06:53,965 --> 00:06:57,900
New compare values load at zero, so no
pulse is cut in half.

92
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:03,070
The same zero event starts the ADC, in the
middle of the all-low vector.

93
00:07:03,110 --> 00:07:07,016
On the bench, bring the ADC trigger out on
a spare pin: on the scope,

94
00:07:07,056 --> 00:07:11,503
it must sit midway through the stretch
where all three poles are low.

95
00:07:12,603 --> 00:07:14,818
Three questions before next time.

96
00:07:14,858 --> 00:07:18,069
One: on a 24 V bus with linear SVPWM,

97
00:07:18,109 --> 00:07:22,387
what's the largest phase-voltage
amplitude?

98
00:07:23,387 --> 00:07:27,943
Two: why are low-side shunts sampled in
the middle of 000?

99
00:07:28,943 --> 00:07:32,914
Three: a current loop with 1 kHz of
bandwidth,

100
00:07:32,954 --> 00:07:39,121
on a motor with L = 0.05 mH. What
proportional gain does it take?

101
00:07:40,121 --> 00:07:42,113
Next time: current control in dq,

102
00:07:42,153 --> 00:07:46,101
with PI controllers tuned straight from
the motor model.
